<h2>The statutory scheme in brief</h2>
<p>Chapter V of the MSMED Act, 2006 builds a complete code for delayed payments to micro and small enterprises: Section 15 obliges the buyer to pay within the agreed period, capped at forty-five days from the day of acceptance or deemed acceptance; Section 16 imposes compound interest at three times the bank rate; Section 17 makes the buyer liable for the amount with interest; and Section 18 supplies the forum — the Micro and Small Enterprises Facilitation Council. Within Section 18, subsections (4) and (5) do quiet but decisive work.</p>

<h2>Section 18(4): the supplier&rsquo;s council</h2>
<p>Section 18(4) provides that the Facilitation Council of the place where the supplier is located has jurisdiction to act as conciliator or arbitrator in a dispute between that supplier and a buyer located anywhere in India. The venue rule is supplier-protective by design: a small enterprise chasing its dues does not have to litigate in the buyer&rsquo;s home State.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For suppliers.</strong> File where you are located — for Delhi-registered units, the Delhi MSEFC — regardless of where the buyer sits or what the purchase order says about courts or venue.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For buyers.</strong> Expect to defend at the supplier&rsquo;s end. Participation before the Council is not a waiver of defences; objections to the claim, the supplier&rsquo;s status or the computation are all raised there.</div>
</div>

<h2>Section 18(5): the ninety-day direction</h2>
<p>Section 18(5) directs that every reference made under the section shall be decided within ninety days from the date of making it. The architecture into which that period fits looks like this:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Reference.</strong> Any party to a dispute over an amount due under Section 17 may refer it to the Council — in practice, through the MSME Samadhaan portal.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Conciliation.</strong> The Council conducts or refers conciliation, applying Sections 65 to 81 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Arbitration.</strong> If conciliation fails, the Council itself arbitrates or refers the dispute to an institution, and the 1996 Act applies as if under a Section 7 arbitration agreement.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Decision.</strong> The reference is to be decided within ninety days of its making — the clock covers the composite process, not the arbitration alone.</div>
</div>
<p>Anyone who practises before Facilitation Councils knows that references frequently outlive the ninety days: conciliation dates, pleadings before the arbitral stage and administrative load all consume time. The lapse of the period does not by itself invalidate the proceedings or the eventual award. The provision&rsquo;s practical value lies elsewhere — it is the yardstick a supplier cites when pressing the Council for dates, and the measure a writ court is reminded of when delay before a Council is challenged as defeating the statute&rsquo;s object.</p>

<h2>What each side should do with these rules</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Suppliers:</strong> keep the Udyam registration, invoices, delivery challans and acceptance trail ready before filing; a documented claim moves faster through both conciliation and arbitration stages.</li>
  <li><strong>Suppliers:</strong> diarise the ninety-day mark and seek expedition in writing when it passes — a paper trail of urgency helps both before the Council and in any later writ proceedings about delay.</li>
  <li><strong>Buyers:</strong> raise jurisdictional and status objections at the first opportunity before the Council itself; sitting out the proceedings only ripens an ex parte award, and a challenge then costs a 75% pre-deposit under Section 19.</li>
  <li><strong>Buyers:</strong> treat the forty-five-day ceiling under Section 15 as a compliance deadline, because interest under Section 16 — compound, at three times the bank rate — accrues automatically and cannot be contracted out of, given Section 24.</li>
  <li><strong>Both sides:</strong> remember that settlement remains open throughout; a conciliated settlement recorded by the Council closes the matter without the pre-deposit and appeal cycle.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>The Delhi picture</h2>
<p>For Delhi suppliers, references go to the Delhi Micro and Small Enterprises Facilitation Council through the Samadhaan portal, and challenges to awards travel to the High Court of Delhi side of Section 34 proceedings with the Section 19 pre-deposit. The venue and timeline rules discussed here are the first things to verify in any delayed-payment brief — they decide where the fight happens and supply the instrument for keeping it moving. This article states the statutory position for general information; the application of these provisions to any particular dispute turns on its own facts and documents.</p>
